Small farms should be given priority when it comes to technological support in Ontario. The research suggests a structural issue rather than a performance issue: small farms are failing because the existing system continuously favors farmers with capital, not because they are badly managed. The current trend of consolidation, which has long-term consequences for rural communities, the variety of the food system, and the fair use of public resources, will be accelerated if large farms continue to get preferential funding. The data presents a convincing narrative. Over the course of the analyzed period, the number of small farms in Ontario has decreased while the size and income of large farms have increased continuously. This is not a coincidence. Higher-earning farms are able to invest in productivity-boosting technology, which increases income and permits more investment. In the meanwhile, small farms must contend with growing input costs, such as labor, gasoline, and fertilizer, which take up a disproportionate amount of their narrower profit margins and leave little money for the type of technological investment that would enable them to compete. Large and small farms are still separated from one another. The structure of the system is the cause of its expansion. Large farms would probably yield higher short-term returns on public investments. There is some substance to the argument. However, it would also intensify the very dynamic that the data already reveals: a food system with less redundancy and resilience, fewer farms, more concentrated ownership, and weakened rural economies. A centralized system has less buffers for the next drought, supply interruption, or shock to commodities prices. This analysis has real uncertainty. Access to technology could not be the only obstacle small farmers must overcome; market access, land prices, and succession issues might all be as restrictive. If that's the case, investment for technology alone won't be enough; it will need to be combined with more extensive structural assistance. If a future pilot program revealed consistently poor technology adoption among small farms even when financing is available, this advice would also need to be reviewed because it would indicate that the obstacle is somewhere else. The following actions are advised in light of this. First, for farms below a certain revenue threshold, create a special small farm technology grant stream rather than loans, which increase debt. Second, since finance without implementation assistance results in limited adoption, any investment should be paired with practical extension support. Third, mandate yearly public reporting on the number of farms by size category and rates of technology use so that the province can keep an eye on whether the intervention is genuinely changing the dynamics of the system. Lastly, commission a cost-benefit study that compares the two investing strategies over a period of 10 and twenty years. While there is a short-term ROI difference between subsidizing small and large farms, the long-term picture—which takes equality, food system resilience, and rural community health into account—probably looks quite different.